<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.10.3: Comparing Functions Represented in Separate Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01027</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.10.3: Comparing Functions Represented in Separate Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>877d54c2-db67-44b4-8e93-461d5bee38bc</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>The number of people who watched a TV episode is a function of that show’s episode number. Here are three graphs of three functions—\(A\), \(B\), and \(C\)—representing three different TV shows.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-align-items-center">
  <para>Show A <figure><media alt="10 data points on coordinate plane."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6d75be6f085a2a634ee4183036bf1fc22c2a49c5"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Show B <figure><media alt="A graph. "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a1912e552a2554e03bb918a73b6894555e02e6c1"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Show C <figure><media alt="10 data points on coordinate plane."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c1cfd27f7b441df073cac25a4cd34a31c1a89ddc"/></media></figure></para>
</div>


<para>Match each description with a graph that could represent the situation described. One of the descriptions has no corresponding graph.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="55e82363-cfbc-47ef-9686-e02aaf346a67" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>This show has a good core audience. It had a guest star in the fifth episode that brought in some new viewers, but most of them stopped watching after that.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph of function \(C\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is: graph of function \(C\)</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="3d080c5a-8054-479a-9f61-4bd9fa4b3120" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>This show is one of the most popular shows, and its audience keeps increasing.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! no match</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is: no match</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="bfa3a562-185d-4ba8-b510-49dbc5bb5223" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>This show has a small audience, but it’s improving, so more people are noticing.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph of function \(A\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is: graph of function \(A\)</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="3c5faec7-0e76-4ed2-a378-ea37bb6269a1" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> This show started out huge. Even though it feels like it crashed, it still has more viewers than another show.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph of function \(B\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is: graph of function \(B\)</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="074f8390-8547-4d20-a6b1-175f0c152fbf" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Which is greatest, \(A(7)\), \(B(7)\), \(C(7)\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(B(7)\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is: \(B(7)\)</para></solution></exercise></section><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Explain what the answer to question 5 tells us about the shows.</para>
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
    3.6 million people watched the 7th episode of Show B.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course to sketch a graph of the viewership of the fourth TV show that did not have a matching graph. 
  </item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-bottom="0" data-left="0" data-right="10" data-schema-version="1.0" data-top="12"/>

